# Negative guards for the certificates module, checked against the standard-preset
# full scan (.tmp/e2e-report-full/findings.json). Same matching rule as the other
# *.deny files: a line matches when a finding's id equals it or starts with
# "<line>:", so a bare rule ID bans every instance.
#
# These two are the reason this file exists. A kind cluster is a kubeadm cluster,
# so it always carries node-bootstrap CertificateSigningRequests: signer
# `kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client-kubelet`, requester `system:bootstrap:<id>`
# or `system:node:<name>`, auto-approved by the controller-manager. If the
# certificates module fires on those, it fires on literally every real cluster on
# day one and the module is noise.
#
# KUBE-CSR-001 is scoped to ServiceAccount requesters precisely so the bootstrap
# flow (never a ServiceAccount) and human cert onboarding fall outside it;
# KUBE-CSR-002 is scoped to the `kubernetes.io/legacy-unknown` signer, which no
# current cluster uses. Neither should have anything to say about a fresh kind
# cluster. Positive coverage for both lives in the corpus case
# testdata/corpus/csr-issuance/, which can construct CSR objects with arbitrary
# requesters without needing an impersonated apply.
KUBE-CSR-001
KUBE-CSR-002
